d/validate]} {:db/id "q-140484" :question/id #uuid "80594410-bd8a-57ed-8b86-b3651312971c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140484" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$\n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{6}{13}-\\frac{6}{13}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{13}+\\frac{9}{13}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9}{13}+\\frac{4}{13}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=4+9=13\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{29}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{4}{29}-\\frac{25}{29}=-1\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140484/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140484"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140485/selection/35f5572ccca3a8c38d3ca06e2a92b51065f48e45d29833e19f4eb153cae096fc" :answer/id #uuid "82fccd32-568c-5947-b890-6cc6e4f75918" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140485/selection/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" :answer/id #uuid "b4247c1b-b6f4-5170-a329-93489cdcc033" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140485/selection/484929341a2d3e721fb79c58b0953e8d8d38c7261c3067d38504f5d555eed643" :answer/id #uuid "cee2d305-15bf-5f7c-962f-68da3579b03d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{19}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140485/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "48c403e5-5750-5f8c-b0f8-ed9677d881fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140485/selection/3a3e10526f40c673b7703a3c103fe7589223997acb2a083d0e3a20b742c55555" :answer/id #uuid "ca65aafc-e741-562a-af9f-cffb83812c18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140485/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "416b5cc6-80b2-5aff-a0fa-8a3d36991ec7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140485/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer-field/choices ["q-140485/selection/35f5572ccca3a8c38d3ca06e2a92b51065f48e45d29833e19f4eb153cae096fc" "q-140485/selection/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" "q-140485/selection/484929341a2d3e721fb79c58b0953e8d8d38c7261c3067d38504f5d555eed643" "q-140485/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" "q-140485/selection/3a3e10526f40c673b7703a3c103fe7589223997acb2a083d0e3a20b742c55555"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140485" :question/id #uuid "96c998e0-f627-54a3-a7d5-35c85c329e46" :question/math-academy-id "q-140485" :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3(\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3})+2{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=4,$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{-5}{1}=-5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140485/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f8f84c-bb94-5f3c-a78e-343f9c0c1b1a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140485"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-83747/selection/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d" :answer/id #uuid "edd2c9ce-cbcd-5d2a-98aa-bccb9c2dc6f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83747/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "e117aadf-3cbf-5cb0-a1ae-14f01bd5ba75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83747/selection/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" :answer/id #uuid "f54ec74f-c3c9-521f-b6f2-660876e2ea3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83747/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" :answer/id #uuid "5fdabf14-a362-5508-af3f-adb65ea0ceae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83747/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "05fb02d2-2c37-5f1f-82b6-83b914aa3df7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83747/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ab297600-23e7-543f-a2eb-aff4fd05fe86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-83747/selection/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d" :answer-field/choices ["q-83747/selection/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d" "q-83747/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-83747/selection/8e918182341451051ce2dca07cfe2b17fa3abe4a925a8df02308f239a4dd798f" "q-83747/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" "q-83747/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83747" :question/id #uuid "713d67ee-e475-52f9-bd91-775ea12c967d" :question/math-academy-id "q-83747" :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{2}}?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-3{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2(-3{x}_{3})+4{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1$, we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{1}{-3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-83747/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f8f84c-bb94-5f3c-a78e-343f9c0c1b1a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83747"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-40297"] :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above, which is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2(2{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-5{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=\\frac{-5}{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-40299/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer/id #uuid "3cbb3e01-53ba-5522-ae46-6ba6420bffe2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40299/selection/8de7a84a41666d4612460439d17949f75cc9ba4883ff75d5034a1786c2a06792" :answer/id #uuid "a66b6833-f86e-5afd-b704-c29951d79d6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40299/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "8229da1f-6f53-5a98-a273-dfffaa426652" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40299/selection/edc901e84f0cdd5174afd49105ac3883566ea0e1e057217fb66083a56cc119ba" :answer/id #uuid "a883cac9-038e-5b26-a479-8558e96e3f18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40299/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "d4276c84-2827-57db-aac7-c6df56241fb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-40299/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7d89e571-4266-582d-8fe4-48a16c2e3884" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-40299/selection/edc901e84f0cdd5174afd49105ac3883566ea0e1e057217fb66083a56cc119ba" :answer-field/choices ["q-40299/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" "q-40299/selection/8de7a84a41666d4612460439d17949f75cc9ba4883ff75d5034a1786c2a06792" "q-40299/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" "q-40299/selection/edc901e84f0cdd5174afd49105ac3883566ea0e1e057217fb66083a56cc119ba" "q-40299/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-40299" :question/id #uuid "7002ea68-32ba-5517-83f4-9ef3c4549a92" :question/math-academy-id "q-40299" :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2},\\,{u}_{3}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$, we need to find a fourth vector $x$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{3}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}+4{x}_{4}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are three pivot columns ($1$st, $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{4}$ is a free variable. From the third and first equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4}$, respectively. Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2}+2(-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4})-{x}_{4}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{5}{x}_{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{4}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{4}=5$, we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\,\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}=-\\frac{1}{5}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-40299/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f8f84c-bb94-5f3c-a78e-343f9c0c1b1a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-40299"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-39480"] :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2},\\,{u}_{3}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$, we need to find a fourth vector $x$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{3}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}-2{x}_{4}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are three pivot columns ($1$st, $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{4}$ is a free variable. From the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=-{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{3}=-2{x}_{4}$, respectively. Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+(-{x}_{4})+(-2{x}_{4})+{x}_{4}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2{x}_{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{4} \\\\ -{x}_{4} \\\\ -2{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{4}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{4}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-81011"] :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{2}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vector of $U$ as ${u}_{1}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$, we need to find a second vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0\\,⟹\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}=\\sqrt{13}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|=|\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}|+|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}|=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{13}}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-81114/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "0e8a6f02-a19d-5421-8f55-754e40af620b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81114/selection/e014eef6d5b5b99f76a63badf4d7e4589d1524a951e6f15c41809dcf478e3da9" :answer/id #uuid "e305a5e3-aba4-5e74-b261-492f3a0ecff1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81114/selection/2edf353dc47d221855e5e7ee4be942392390ff01766e089c29dc368c2051b12d" :answer/id #uuid "84ec0d37-6054-5896-949d-6ea84abf3e91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81114/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "5eca89dc-ea14-5e21-91f0-e71ae4acfba0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81114/selection/af5580a233ee3709064249d6732fc822dfb93f38caf90c025deb51b127e90b2c" :answer/id #uuid "5ac70841-1b79-5a4d-911f-627e1e3e8ce6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81114/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3db9a2ce-3526-5707-a596-4c81688f0223" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-81114/selection/2edf353dc47d221855e5e7ee4be942392390ff01766e089c29dc368c2051b12d" :answer-field/choices ["q-81114/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-81114/selection/e014eef6d5b5b99f76a63badf4d7e4589d1524a951e6f15c41809dcf478e3da9" "q-81114/selection/2edf353dc47d221855e5e7ee4be942392390ff01766e089c29dc368c2051b12d" "q-81114/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-81114/selection/af5580a233ee3709064249d6732fc822dfb93f38caf90c025deb51b127e90b2c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81114" :question/id #uuid "0fca8409-6035-5535-87d2-9ec23d0bda51" :question/math-academy-id "q-81114" :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{2}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vector of $U$ as ${u}_{1}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$, we need to find a second vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|=|-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-81114/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "baf333d2-6bd9-5d79-9c07-85856c81672a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81114"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140488/selection/34fd0d0bd76ef1ea2bb01e196c59d2fd831d82deccea3606e50fc340e8599046" :answer/id #uuid "2b07817c-b525-52e4-981a-0355986a107c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140488/selection/8ecb517b344adfb5d1a1435d81f147224ba4a1b6088d276547ca51a9d7134f0c" :answer/id #uuid "7e6f66f0-0201-5da6-9e95-55bcb67ca9fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140488/selection/626defb5b5e56dc8dc9e37e0e8bb75f093735423836176f7d8564d0b4fefa0c8" :answer/id #uuid "8f50d622-038f-590b-aaa8-6841e0353f57" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140488/selection/2e749d43f32020367d5726e92adc3f0814314dd4fcec1e71668c3fb11f471d74" :answer/id #uuid "918e0271-4f3b-55fa-89be-02e7a9a79184" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140488/selection/28134a524827c06a5643fadc5de6fef46c6c404f59ca44b6b1b47bafec13357e" :answer/id #uuid "cb886aa7-99d2-556e-b80b-c2e7f12c3f8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140488/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ebea7b1a-601a-5ebf-9ee6-4a51cddaaacd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140488/selection/626defb5b5e56dc8dc9e37e0e8bb75f093735423836176f7d8564d0b4fefa0c8" :answer-field/choices ["q-140488/selection/34fd0d0bd76ef1ea2bb01e196c59d2fd831d82deccea3606e50fc340e8599046" "q-140488/selection/8ecb517b344adfb5d1a1435d81f147224ba4a1b6088d276547ca51a9d7134f0c" "q-140488/selection/626defb5b5e56dc8dc9e37e0e8bb75f093735423836176f7d8564d0b4fefa0c8" "q-140488/selection/2e749d43f32020367d5726e92adc3f0814314dd4fcec1e71668c3fb11f471d74" "q-140488/selection/28134a524827c06a5643fadc5de6fef46c6c404f59ca44b6b1b47bafec13357e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140488" :question/id #uuid "4c1907b8-bbf2-5329-b7a6-f92c1f1333cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-140488" :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{2}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vector of $U$ as ${u}_{1}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$, we need to find a second vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equation, we get ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{10}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|=|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}|=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{10}}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140488/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "baf333d2-6bd9-5d79-9c07-85856c81672a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140488"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-81117/selection/787c3950c6b72b59087094f00cae4bb1937807004aeb227a4998f242ce6a8c1b" :answer/id #uuid "e112f56d-f0b5-559f-8257-c193201d8019" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{35}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81117/selection/dc150573fefdd11fbd745700ca0338993024fbaa0475bbd32592045814aa092a" :answer/id #uuid "1f3ab3b5-451d-53f5-a0f2-2779bcf2cca0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{35}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81117/selection/d78b54f58fcf7c4aa80da8def8c0067e3e873f040d9814d091370f7dc1f27d20" :answer/id #uuid "1ad2a5d3-d161-5ec8-8ae6-08ca6e04e7af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{7}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81117/selection/874711e508a4453623f4b73f110a23c0d2a5d6c6653c42ed4d3e3e926517451a" :answer/id #uuid "b1dab33a-7cbd-5790-aa68-54902b5db5ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81117/selection/a1462f701bdeaccd73b0e04a51e62888e75b7a078714943c7475b23400f2ed26" :answer/id #uuid "75352235-fe8d-56c5-9d46-b59c2683f340" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{35}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81117/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5c42b2df-8d3c-5263-82f9-eed267c80263" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-81117/selection/787c3950c6b72b59087094f00cae4bb1937807004aeb227a4998f242ce6a8c1b" :answer-field/choices ["q-81117/selection/787c3950c6b72b59087094f00cae4bb1937807004aeb227a4998f242ce6a8c1b" "q-81117/selection/dc150573fefdd11fbd745700ca0338993024fbaa0475bbd32592045814aa092a" "q-81117/selection/d78b54f58fcf7c4aa80da8def8c0067e3e873f040d9814d091370f7dc1f27d20" "q-81117/selection/874711e508a4453623f4b73f110a23c0d2a5d6c6653c42ed4d3e3e926517451a" "q-81117/selection/a1462f701bdeaccd73b0e04a51e62888e75b7a078714943c7475b23400f2ed26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81117" :question/id #uuid "fd7e2b49-e015-5da5-97ce-63ab84487079" :question/math-academy-id "q-81117" :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\sqrt{10}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\sqrt{14}\\cdot {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=3{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-2{x}_{1}+3(3{x}_{3})+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=5{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{35}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|=|\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{35}}|+|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{35}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{35}}|=\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{35}}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-81117/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "baf333d2-6bd9-5d79-9c07-85856c81672a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81117"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-83763/selection/79a5c788de3cafd018b67b13dd70e858d2fe75a5a4d6cb7f32064ed68bd3a8cb" :answer/id #uuid "3118939f-07c0-5ccf-9d12-b1e154fbf294" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{30}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83763/selection/4319c77bb2fb3c0b2025819ca3a220e5d4dbfb82a7b501e88477b0f23ddba7a1" :answer/id #uuid "b64428af-a427-5c84-8fb9-3e5fa1cbfb73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{30}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83763/selection/5d497bd88b0a28faec6e160b130e1c807e1738d54253821d1011fe9d1d71f1a4" :answer/id #uuid "5f48638b-b8ea-5bf5-9c25-817e4a616ca7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83763/selection/ecfd32530d06fcdda734ea7dd023dc55de7cf3c78ee93ff91e3d0afb40666587" :answer/id #uuid "de3a844e-3797-5f24-8e94-1791bf1e7ad5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{30}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83763/selection/05cd0f46b43fcbc8dc3c6af59cb724e9d105887a7e692b359114cfd6d114e90a" :answer/id #uuid "9b93219c-eb15-54af-b13e-09f4cf658f3d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{30}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83763/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e2d55033-2007-5436-a931-a60a79f0c1f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-83763/selection/4319c77bb2fb3c0b2025819ca3a220e5d4dbfb82a7b501e88477b0f23ddba7a1" :answer-field/choices ["q-83763/selection/79a5c788de3cafd018b67b13dd70e858d2fe75a5a4d6cb7f32064ed68bd3a8cb" "q-83763/selection/4319c77bb2fb3c0b2025819ca3a220e5d4dbfb82a7b501e88477b0f23ddba7a1" "q-83763/selection/5d497bd88b0a28faec6e160b130e1c807e1738d54253821d1011fe9d1d71f1a4" "q-83763/selection/ecfd32530d06fcdda734ea7dd023dc55de7cf3c78ee93ff91e3d0afb40666587" "q-83763/selection/05cd0f46b43fcbc8dc3c6af59cb724e9d105887a7e692b359114cfd6d114e90a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83763" :question/id #uuid "ccf01290-02e2-5c4e-9863-5d516274aa67" :question/math-academy-id "q-83763" :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\sqrt{5}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\sqrt{6}\\cdot {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3},$ and from the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-5)^{2}+{2}^{2}}=\\sqrt{30}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|=|-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}|+|-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}|+|\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}}|=\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{30}}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-83763/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "baf333d2-6bd9-5d79-9c07-85856c81672a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83763"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7257"] :question/problem "Determine whether the following set is orthogonal:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/8f316d4e8c33c1d8d087f2fb42e283169919a4cbc9c14a67792df70a447a41b9.png)" :question/worked-solution "If we denote the vectors in our set ${X}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\mathbf{x}_3 \\},$ we can check that ${X}$ is orthogonal by showing that \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute each dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2=0 & ✓ \\\\ {x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 0=2\\ne 0 & \\times  \\\\ {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=(-1)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 0=-1\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo ${X}$ is *not* an orthogonal set." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5563"] :question/problem "Determine whether the following set is orthonormal:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/7e263f3e5b4f2491f2f5c469a3a0f861bf2985e67fec67d3016f4bb7b53f4493.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given set as $\\{\\mathbf{w}_1,\\mathbf{w}_2\\},$ respectively. \n\nThe set $W$ is orthonormal if the following conditions hold:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/1a2b4f5d6fb8842115feb6e6f12ac70399768c94006966838c3817eabd85e7fb.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our set:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=16+16=32\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $\\mathbf w_1$ and $\\mathbf w_2$ are orthogonal, and $\\mathbf w_1$ is a unit vector, but $\\mathbf w_2$ is *not* a unit vector.\n\nTherefore, ${W}$ is *not* an orthonormal set." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5564"] :question/problem "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $\\mathbf x = [x_1,\\,x_2,\\,x_3]^T,$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/b62da31df7bea27c23f8dfcf1615ae9514ed48727e8503a389abc9a55ce1d108.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of ${U}$ as $\\mathbf{u}_1,\\, \\mathbf{u}_2.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ we need to find a third vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/95c6967179c825684229dcdd991bc0ba13d66d2f7fa44d3a4f62b9c54092e229.png) such that\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/410f07f748fa4e40decedadacd97ad7df0b5f9201c53db8f4934fd3096aea470.png)\n\nSo, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $3$rd). This means $x_2$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_3=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/0477864853171cf0afdcc87e2debb27c24efa8b6166cc396d8f7cd0a126f7ace.png)\n\nSetting $x_2=1,$ we obtain the vector \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/a6e3e898672afd315ae56af8a6e098565947b3cf8c7c2e86792e27e8e8775cdb.png)\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/87b1cb524aee7db1f7e6f2a34c81874d2f9dae66d94a4e7b8b5c63b3713d9da7.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7114"] :question/problem "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $\\mathbf x = [x_1,\\,x_2,\\,x_3]^T,$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/969339570bc776ebf69a7f6e9cbff0377fa35842a913e68680d8bd2ed0a1decb.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of ${U}$ as $\\mathbf{u}_1,\\, \\mathbf{u}_2.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ we need to find a third vector $\\mathbf{x}$ such that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1$ and\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/e7034bdd15c1adc9aca80284bb2ab9b5a99ce5f33fa65f7b867c542158727b9d.png)\n\nSo, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\sqrt{6}\\cdot {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). This means $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get $x_2=-x_3,$ and from the first equation, we obtain $x_1=x_3.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/e690911497bbee717323b680e92c57a7ab9d6d9d78d6ed3440d2657cd5883c3d.png)\n\nSetting $x_3=1,$ we obtain the vector \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/53cd895629e3c850067d8e443c5f7ce411d1a9358a6123bc72465fdce673f14c.png) \n\nNow, we need to normalize $\\mathbf{x}.$ Notice that $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = \\sqrt{1^2+(-1)^2+1^2} = \\sqrt{3}.$ So, we normalize $\\mathbf x$ as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/86d8735a9101ad90efe6c83e21ba6849f488baf3202ff549e9aea92764019c0e.png)\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065835/assets/24cd2c6c9a445179df93ecf534fbab1db3906a6db78473505ea0fd6c7de2e466.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
